{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>פָּקֹד יִפְקֹד</b> [God will surely visit]. The reason for the doubling is to validate the reliability of the matter. And this is its measure: ״פָּקֹד״ — meaning, the appointed remembrance that HaShem promised will surely come — ״יִפְקֹד״. It further intends to allude to two things: to the removal of harm, which is their deliverance from the affliction of Egypt and from the yoke of their burden, and to the drawing near of benefit — that they would go out with great abundance for the house of Israel, as it is written (Genesis 15:14): ״וְאַחֲרֵי כֵן יֵצְאוּ בִּרְכוּשׁ גָּדוֹל״. And the reason he alluded to this matter to them is because it touches upon the oath, so that this matter would not become a cause for them to refrain from taking up his bones, on account of their being preoccupied with gathering wealth and loading silver and gold, since it would be so abundant — as they said, of blessed memory (Bechorot 8a), that there was no person in Israel who did not load ten donkeys' worth of silver and gold of Egypt, and this would be a cause for preventing the matter. Therefore he said <b>the oath</b> etc. <b>saying: פָּקֹד יִפְקֹד</b> — including also the bestowal of wealth, and even so you shall press yourselves and bear my bones with you, even though there will result from this, by that measure, a loss of a load's worth of silver and gold. And you will find that this is what Moses did, as they said, of blessed memory (Shemot Rabbah, ch. 20), that in the place where he would have loaded silver and gold he loaded the coffin of Joseph in its stead, and he took his reward for the matter." "<b>From this with you.</b> The reason he said <b>מִזֶּה</b> [from this] is not to say that they would specifically bring him up only from there, but rather he intended to strengthen the oath so that it would be firmly binding, according to the law of one who swears upon the understanding of his fellow for a kindness that he did for him. And therefore he stipulated and said <b>מִזֶּה</b> — meaning, according to what they said, of blessed memory (Bereshit Rabbah, ch. 84), on the verse ״נָסְעוּ מִזֶּה״ — they departed from the brotherly love, which is the numerical value of זֶה (zeh, twelve). And this is what he alluded to them here at the time of the oath in saying <b>מִזֶּה אִתְּכֶם</b> — meaning, that through the very kindness for which they are swearing to him, by which they removed him from here and departed from his brotherhood and did what they did to him — now the matter will be repaired and they will be as one. And this is his saying ״אִתְּכֶם״ — they will encamp from the journeying to be with him, with them, and that sin will be uprooted, and behold they are together in brotherhood. And the intention in this is that he forgave them for how they had offended him regarding this kindness for which they now swear to him; and accordingly, behold they are swearing upon his understanding, for the kindness he did for them."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :register {:flags {0 ["God"]}}, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:27:43.337794253Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}